1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:06,615
Have you ever wondered how a rover could learn a useful route
without someone writing every turn?

2
00:00:06,785 --> 00:00:14,614
Imagine a simulated rover choosing between two paths, trying
moves, and receiving points for the outcomes.

3
00:00:14,784 --> 00:00:19,712
The important question is how that experience changes its next
decision.

4
00:00:19,882 --> 00:00:26,389
Keep your Blue Impulse study guide open at page twenty-five. We
will work directly through the book.

5
00:00:26,559 --> 00:00:33,513
By the end, you will calculate a learning update yourself and
explain what the result actually means.

6
00:00:34,633 --> 00:00:41,887
Today you will identify an agent, an environment, a state, an
action, a reward, and a policy.

7
00:00:42,057 --> 00:00:49,118
You will compare exploration with exploitation, then work
through the numerical example on page twenty-eight.

8
00:00:49,288 --> 00:00:54,472
Welcome to the tutorial on basic reinforcement learning by Blue
Impulse.

9
00:00:54,642 --> 00:01:02,471
Have paper ready for one calculation. The tutorial PDF includes
the same worked steps and practice question.

10
00:01:02,641 --> 00:01:11,089
Treat our rover as an illustration. We are explaining a learning
method, not claiming every real rover learns this way.

11
00:01:12,233 --> 00:01:17,759
Look at the diagram on page twenty-five, titled One interaction
at time t.

12
00:01:17,929 --> 00:01:24,585
The agent selects an action. The environment changes and returns
the next state and a reward.

13
00:01:24,755 --> 00:01:32,456
The learning algorithm uses that experience to improve the
policy, which is the strategy for choosing actions.

14
00:01:32,626 --> 00:01:38,023
For our grid, the state could be the current square, and an
action could be moving right.

15
00:01:38,193 --> 00:01:45,425
The reward is a number associated with the result. It is
feedback, not a complete instruction manual.

16
00:01:45,595 --> 00:01:51,590
Notice the return arrow. Learning depends on connecting an
action to what happened after it.

17
00:01:52,733 --> 00:01:59,731
Page twenty-six separates three ideas that are easy to confuse:
reward, policy, and value.

18
00:01:59,901 --> 00:02:06,258
Think of a journey. The reward scores an outcome now, while the
policy chooses the next move.

19
00:02:06,428 --> 00:02:12,615
A value estimate predicts how much return a choice may lead to,
including later outcomes.

20
00:02:12,785 --> 00:02:19,142
A route with a small cost now can still be worthwhile if it
reaches a better destination.

21
00:02:19,312 --> 00:02:25,797
That is why choosing the biggest immediate reward is not always
the best long-term decision.

22
00:02:25,967 --> 00:02:33,477
The book also warns that poorly chosen rewards can encourage
shortcuts that miss the designer's real goal.

23
00:02:34,600 --> 00:02:38,995
Turn to page twenty-seven and use the book's restaurant analogy.

24
00:02:39,165 --> 00:02:46,759
Ordering the dish you currently believe is best is exploitation.
Trying a different dish is exploration.

25
00:02:46,929 --> 00:02:52,433
Exploration gathers information because your current estimates
might be wrong.

26
00:02:52,603 --> 00:03:00,945
In the book's epsilon-greedy example, epsilon is zero point one,
giving a ten percent exploration probability.

27
00:03:01,115 --> 00:03:05,957
Otherwise, the agent chooses an action with the highest current
Q value.

28
00:03:06,127 --> 00:03:15,045
These percentages describe probabilities over choices, not a
promise that exactly one out of every ten choices explores.

29
00:03:15,215 --> 00:03:24,047
In a real physical system, exploration also needs safety
constraints. A simulation is a useful place to experiment.

30
00:03:25,167 --> 00:03:30,820
Pause and think. One route has worked twice. Another route has
never been tried.

31
00:03:30,990 --> 00:03:36,601
Does that prove the first route is better? Take a few seconds
before we reveal the answer.

32
00:03:41,771 --> 00:03:47,723
No. The evidence is incomplete, so the familiar route is not
certainly the better one.

33
00:03:47,893 --> 00:03:55,765
Exploration may improve the estimate, but it has a cost. The
design must balance information and performance.

34
00:03:55,935 --> 00:04:04,319
Write one sentence explaining why exploitation uses estimated
future return, rather than only the next reward.

35
00:04:05,467 --> 00:04:14,021
Now open page twenty-eight, Update a Q value. Q associates a
state and an action with an estimated return.

36
00:04:14,191 --> 00:04:21,914
The book calls Q-learning model-free because it does not require
a supplied model of environment transitions.

37
00:04:22,084 --> 00:04:29,337
Model-free does not mean memory-free. The estimates still live
in a table or another representation.

38
00:04:29,507 --> 00:04:34,969
The update combines an old estimate with a target derived from
new experience.

39
00:04:35,139 --> 00:04:42,947
Alpha controls how far the estimate moves toward that target.
Gamma discounts the future-value contribution.

40
00:04:43,117 --> 00:04:49,005
We will use exactly the numbers in the guide, so you can check
each line against the page.

41
00:04:50,133 --> 00:04:56,256
Start with an old Q value of two and a learning rate, alpha, of
zero point five.

42
00:04:56,426 --> 00:05:02,335
The reward is one, gamma is zero point nine, and the best next Q
value is four.

43
00:05:02,505 --> 00:05:09,716
First calculate the target: one plus zero point nine times four.
The target is four point six.

44
00:05:09,886 --> 00:05:16,819
Next subtract the old estimate. Four point six minus two gives
an error of two point six.

45
00:05:16,989 --> 00:05:22,515
Multiply that error by alpha. Half of two point six is one point
three.

46
00:05:22,685 --> 00:05:28,999
Finally add the change to the old value. Two plus one point
three gives three point three.

47
00:05:29,169 --> 00:05:36,913
Watch the estimate move halfway toward the target. That halfway
movement is what alpha equals one half means here.

48
00:05:37,083 --> 00:05:44,763
The supporting Python file repeats this calculation and prints
every intermediate value for you to inspect.

49
00:05:45,900 --> 00:05:54,241
Return to the final paragraph on page twenty-eight. This
experience raised the estimate from two to three point three.

50
00:05:54,411 --> 00:06:01,473
It did not prove that the action is always best. One observation
is not a reliable policy.

51
00:06:01,643 --> 00:06:08,213
Repeated experience, an appropriate learning schedule, and
enough exploration are still important.

52
00:06:08,383 --> 00:06:14,826
There is also a special case: when the next state is terminal,
the future-value term is zero.

53
00:06:14,996 --> 00:06:20,991
That prevents the calculation from inventing future reward after
the episode has ended.

54
00:06:21,161 --> 00:06:28,883
Compare these two ideas: updating an estimate from evidence, and
guaranteeing the outcome of the next action.

55
00:06:29,053 --> 00:06:33,853
They are different claims, and only the first follows from our
calculation.

56
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:40,675
Your turn. Keep the old value at two, alpha at one half, and the
reward at one.

57
00:06:40,845 --> 00:06:47,181
Make the next state terminal. What is the new Q value? Pause the
video if you need more time.

58
00:06:53,351 --> 00:07:00,881
The future contribution is zero, so the target is one. The error
is one minus two, or negative one.

59
00:07:01,051 --> 00:07:07,985
Half that error is negative one half. Add it to two, giving a
new value of one point five.

60
00:07:08,155 --> 00:07:16,283
The estimate falls because the observed target is lower. A
learning update can decrease a value as well as increase it.

61
00:07:17,433 --> 00:07:22,511
Let's recap using pages twenty-five through twenty-eight. The
agent acts,

62
00:07:22,621 --> 00:07:26,461
receives feedback, and learns a policy from experience.

63
00:07:26,631 --> 00:07:33,308
Reward scores an outcome. Value estimates return. Exploration
improves information,

64
00:07:33,418 --> 00:07:36,938
while exploitation uses the best current estimates.

65
00:07:37,108 --> 00:07:44,169
Our worked Q update produced three point three, and our
terminal-state practice produced one point five.

66
00:07:44,339 --> 00:07:53,129
Page thirty-one adds an important distinction: running an
already learned policy is not the same as training it online.

67
00:07:53,299 --> 00:07:58,248
A fixed sensor-response rule can control a machine without
learning at all.

68
00:07:58,418 --> 00:08:06,375
Before moving on, explain the loop to someone else and run the
included calculation with one changed reward.

69
00:08:07,500 --> 00:08:15,329
This tutorial was taught from the Blue Impulse study guide,
especially pages twenty-four to twenty-eight and thirty-one.

70
00:08:15,499 --> 00:08:19,254
The cover on screen is the original cover of your book.

71
00:08:19,424 --> 00:08:28,491
Use the companion tutorial PDF, narration script, subtitles, and
Python demonstration to practise at your own pace.

72
00:08:28,661 --> 00:08:33,887
Blue Impulse study guide. Learn the reasoning, then test it with
an example.
